1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,691
Here is a question for a model:
what cheese is in this photo?

2
00:00:04,011 --> 00:00:09,323
Both answers on screen come
from the same predict code in cli.py.

3
00:00:09,643 --> 00:00:11,307
And both answers say brie.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:12,937
Now count the numbers.

5
00:00:13,257 --> 00:00:16,115
The top answer,
the README output, holds three.

6
00:00:16,435 --> 00:00:19,145
The bottom one,
from this recording, holds four.

7
00:00:19,465 --> 00:00:21,321
One model cannot give both.

8
00:00:21,641 --> 00:00:25,246
So which model is behind an endpoint,
and what else is there?

9
00:00:25,483 --> 00:00:29,581
Behind an endpoint sit three pieces:
an artifact, which model;

10
00:00:29,581 --> 00:00:34,208
a container, what runs it;
and a machine, where it runs, and who pays.

11
00:00:34,528 --> 00:00:38,326
The meter beside the machine keeps running
while no request arrives.

12
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:42,805
In part 2,
we stop running these steps by hand.

13
00:00:43,125 --> 00:00:46,490
The four stages of the Cheese App
become one pipeline

14
00:00:46,490 --> 00:00:48,267
that the cloud runs for you.

15
00:00:48,517 --> 00:00:52,748
To follow along,
you need an AWS account with billing,

16
00:00:52,748 --> 00:00:56,666
the AWS CLI, Docker,
and both repositories cloned.

17
00:00:56,986 --> 00:00:59,631
This is our first lecture on AWS.

18
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:02,701
A tracker in the corner
names the README step,

19
00:01:02,701 --> 00:01:05,904
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

20
00:01:06,224 --> 00:01:08,763
Every command is also in the study guide.

21
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:11,892
First, where this lecture sits.

22
00:01:12,212 --> 00:01:14,855
The course project
is in its prototype phase,

23
00:01:14,855 --> 00:01:18,143
and of the four parts of the Cheese App,
three are done.

24
00:01:18,463 --> 00:01:20,297
Model deploy is still open.

25
00:01:20,533 --> 00:01:22,816
Part 1 of this video fills that tile.

26
00:01:23,136 --> 00:01:24,864
Part 2 connects all four.

27
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:28,769
Last week,
lecture 14 put containers on Cloud Run.

28
00:01:29,089 --> 00:01:31,713
Today, the container serves a trained model.

29
00:01:31,950 --> 00:01:35,044
The lecture maps its console
onto the course:

30
00:01:35,044 --> 00:01:37,520
notebooks, training from lecture 13,

31
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,889
and today's two,
online prediction and pipelines.

32
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:44,674
The lecture is written
for Vertex AI on Google Cloud.

33
00:01:44,994 --> 00:01:49,036
This course also ships an AWS
version of both tutorials,

34
00:01:49,036 --> 00:01:51,202
and that is the one we follow.

35
00:01:51,522 --> 00:01:53,442
Same ideas, different names.

36
00:01:53,762 --> 00:01:55,768
I'll point out each one as we meet it.

37
00:01:56,017 --> 00:01:58,342
A Google Cloud bucket becomes S3.

38
00:01:58,662 --> 00:02:03,264
A service account key becomes an IAM user
with a key on your laptop,

39
00:02:03,264 --> 00:02:04,956
plus a role in the cloud.

40
00:02:05,276 --> 00:02:09,030
And an endpoint becomes
a configuration plus an endpoint.

41
00:02:09,267 --> 00:02:13,342
One thing about this recording:
it has no AWS account.

42
00:02:13,662 --> 00:02:16,392
Every step that runs on your laptop is real.

43
00:02:16,712 --> 00:02:21,150
Every AWS call stops
at its honest first error, like this one.

44
00:02:21,470 --> 00:02:23,184
Where the cloud would answer,

45
00:02:23,184 --> 00:02:26,376
you see the README output,
or a tagged local stand-in.

46
00:02:26,696 --> 00:02:28,232
Never a faked success.

47
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:31,219
Versions at the time
of recording are on screen.

48
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:35,756
Step 1: clone the repository.

49
00:02:36,076 --> 00:02:40,172
The README says clone or download,
so our git clone is extra,

50
00:02:40,172 --> 00:02:43,799
and so is the git log
that shows the commit we follow.

51
00:02:44,119 --> 00:02:45,378
Pause and clone it.

52
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:49,522
An extra listing:
the CLI, a Dockerfile, a Pipfile,

53
00:02:49,522 --> 00:02:54,193
eight sample photos,
and docker-shell.sh, already executable.

54
00:02:54,434 --> 00:02:57,591
Steps 2 and 3 are in the AWS console.

55
00:02:57,911 --> 00:03:01,474
Check that you can use
S3, SageMaker and IAM.

56
00:03:01,794 --> 00:03:06,615
Then create an IAM user called
model-deployment, with three policies.

57
00:03:06,935 --> 00:03:09,154
The third is broader than this code needs.

58
00:03:09,474 --> 00:03:11,607
Create access keys and save both.

59
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:16,250
Step 4 creates a role named
SageMakerExecutionRole,

60
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:20,833
trusted by SageMaker,
with the SageMaker and S3 policies.

61
00:03:21,153 --> 00:03:22,561
Copy its ARN.

62
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,208
Why two identities?

63
00:03:24,528 --> 00:03:27,779
Your laptop signs each request
with the user key,

64
00:03:27,779 --> 00:03:31,163
and the endpoint you create
later acts as the role.

65
00:03:31,483 --> 00:03:33,232
The key stays on your laptop.

66
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:37,830
Step 5, then pause:
create a bucket with a name of your own,

67
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:41,467
because bucket names are unique
across all of AWS.

68
00:03:41,787 --> 00:03:44,027
Storage bills while files sit in it.

69
00:03:44,347 --> 00:03:47,675
And as the README warns,
never put keys in a script,

70
00:03:47,675 --> 00:03:49,531
and never commit them to Git.

71
00:03:49,767 --> 00:03:53,202
Run Container, step 1: aws configure.

72
00:03:53,522 --> 00:03:54,972
Then come four prompts.

73
00:03:55,292 --> 00:03:56,807
You paste your two keys.

74
00:03:57,127 --> 00:04:02,055
We have none, so we press Enter twice,
then type us-east-1 and json.

75
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:06,543
Two extras: only the config file exists,
since we typed no keys,

76
00:04:06,543 --> 00:04:10,257
and aws sts get-caller-identity
cannot find credentials.

77
00:04:10,577 --> 00:04:12,689
With your keys, it prints your account.

78
00:04:13,009 --> 00:04:14,738
Pause and make yours print it.

79
00:04:14,984 --> 00:04:18,781
Step 2 offers a second option:
three exports in your shell.

80
00:04:19,101 --> 00:04:21,853
AWS rejects our placeholder key.

81
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,640
There is a subtler gap:
the script passes only the region,

82
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:28,265
the bucket and the role into the container.

83
00:04:28,585 --> 00:04:30,783
So exported keys fall off at the wall.

84
00:04:31,103 --> 00:04:34,004
Use option 1, or add the keys with -e.

85
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:40,757
Step 3:
edit docker-shell.sh with your bucket,

86
00:04:40,757 --> 00:04:43,584
the ARN of your role, and your region.

87
00:04:43,904 --> 00:04:45,440
Ours are placeholders.

88
00:04:45,833 --> 00:04:48,308
Step 4, chmod, prints nothing.

89
00:04:48,628 --> 00:04:52,404
An extra listing shows
the script was already executable.

90
00:04:52,650 --> 00:04:55,530
Step 5: sh docker-shell.sh.

91
00:04:55,850 --> 00:05:01,299
It pulls the base image for arm64,
then stops at the first RUN line:

92
00:05:01,299 --> 00:05:02,741
exec format error.

93
00:05:03,061 --> 00:05:06,367
Our Intel style laptop
cannot run those programs.

94
00:05:06,616 --> 00:05:08,152
The fix is in the script.

95
00:05:08,472 --> 00:05:11,150
Line 19 is for Apple silicon Macs,

96
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:14,851
and line 17,
commented out, is the plain build.

97
00:05:15,171 --> 00:05:17,197
Swap them: fix 1, an extra.

98
00:05:17,517 --> 00:05:20,333
On Apple silicon the original line works.

99
00:05:20,653 --> 00:05:23,555
Elsewhere it means slow emulation,
or this error.

100
00:05:23,816 --> 00:05:25,309
Now look at the Pipfile.

101
00:05:25,629 --> 00:05:30,536
TensorFlow is pinned to one version,
but SageMaker says "*": any version.

102
00:05:30,856 --> 00:05:34,195
And no lock is used:
Git ignores the lock file,

103
00:05:34,195 --> 00:05:37,747
and the Dockerfile runs
pipenv lock at build time.

104
00:05:37,983 --> 00:05:39,327
Something to ponder.

105
00:05:39,647 --> 00:05:43,060
The code worked when it was written,
and nobody changed a line.

106
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:46,388
Why does cli.py fail at import today?

107
00:05:55,233 --> 00:05:58,561
"*" is a constraint,
not a pin, as in lecture 1:

108
00:05:58,561 --> 00:06:02,166
the resolver takes the newest release
that installs.

109
00:06:02,486 --> 00:06:05,964
At the time of recording, on Python 3.9,

110
00:06:05,964 --> 00:06:11,788
that is SageMaker SDK version 3,
with PyTorch and 14 CUDA packages.

111
00:06:12,033 --> 00:06:17,796
Version 3 is a rewritten package,
without the modules cli.py imports:

112
00:06:17,796 --> 00:06:22,806
no tensorflow module, no serializers,
no get_execution_role.

113
00:06:23,126 --> 00:06:24,641
The imports find nothing.

114
00:06:24,883 --> 00:06:28,915
Here, the unpinned build ran 13 minutes,
then ran out of disk.

115
00:06:29,235 --> 00:06:33,032
On your laptop it would finish,
then fail at the first import.

116
00:06:33,266 --> 00:06:36,999
Fix 2, also an extra: change "*" to "<3".

117
00:06:37,319 --> 00:06:40,381
The resolver stops
at the last version 2 release,

118
00:06:40,381 --> 00:06:41,693
and the imports work.

119
00:06:42,013 --> 00:06:43,784
Pause and make both fixes.

120
00:06:45,316 --> 00:06:46,767
Run step 5 again.

121
00:06:47,087 --> 00:06:51,780
It builds in almost 7 minutes,
mostly locking and installing packages.

122
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:56,324
The AWS check fails without keys,
but the shell still opens.

123
00:06:56,566 --> 00:07:02,767
Inside, extras show Python 3.9,
SageMaker version 2 and TensorFlow 2.18,

124
00:07:02,767 --> 00:07:05,953
and the three values from the script.

125
00:07:06,273 --> 00:07:08,726
Deprecation warnings are not the error.

126
00:07:08,966 --> 00:07:13,680
A small slip: the help text
still says GCS bucket and Vertex AI,

127
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:15,963
and the test flag does nothing.

128
00:07:16,283 --> 00:07:18,630
The commands are the AWS ones.

129
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:21,169
The image is about 4 gigabytes.

130
00:07:22,517 --> 00:07:26,328
Before we prepare the model,
a design question from the lecture:

131
00:07:26,328 --> 00:07:28,234
where does preprocessing happen?

132
00:07:28,554 --> 00:07:31,797
In a notebook,
you resize and normalize cell by cell.

133
00:07:32,117 --> 00:07:34,905
A production endpoint
should take the raw photo

134
00:07:34,905 --> 00:07:36,448
and do all of that itself.

135
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:39,404
In this tutorial, the client does it.

136
00:07:39,724 --> 00:07:43,200
The predict code opens the photo,

137
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:48,257
converts it to RGB
and resizes it to 224 by 224.

138
00:07:48,577 --> 00:07:53,442
Then it divides
by 255 and sends the numbers as JSON.

139
00:07:53,684 --> 00:07:58,591
An extra check reads the model's front door,
which TensorFlow calls a signature.

140
00:07:58,911 --> 00:08:03,916
It takes floats,
shaped any number by 224 by 224 by 3,

141
00:08:03,916 --> 00:08:06,975
and gives four numbers per photo.

142
00:08:07,295 --> 00:08:09,982
Preprocessing sits on your side of the wire.

143
00:08:10,217 --> 00:08:13,396
The lecture names the risk:
client-side brittleness.

144
00:08:13,716 --> 00:08:17,188
Every client must repeat
those steps exactly,

145
00:08:17,188 --> 00:08:22,590
and a phone app that forgets to divide
by 255 gets confident nonsense.

146
00:08:22,910 --> 00:08:27,348
One copy inside the model
is also easier to roll back and version.

147
00:08:27,584 --> 00:08:30,656
The fix from the lecture sits
in the part 2 trainer.

148
00:08:30,976 --> 00:08:36,266
The preprocess function decodes the JPEG,
converts it to floats, and resizes it.

149
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:41,110
A function marked as the model's front
door takes a batch of JPEG strings,

150
00:08:41,110 --> 00:08:43,540
prepares each one, and calls the model.

151
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:45,972
It is saved as the serving_default.

152
00:08:46,292 --> 00:08:48,874
Raw bytes in, one score per class out.

153
00:08:49,117 --> 00:08:50,546
One trap to avoid.

154
00:08:50,866 --> 00:08:55,538
The README says the prepare step converts
the model to the SavedModel format.

155
00:08:55,858 --> 00:08:57,458
That is a change of format,

156
00:08:57,458 --> 00:09:00,125
not the change of signature
from the lecture.

157
00:09:01,467 --> 00:09:05,307
Step 1: python cli.py --prepare.

158
00:09:05,627 --> 00:09:07,312
The README lists four things.

159
00:09:07,632 --> 00:09:12,804
Download the model from GitHub,
convert it to the SavedModel format,

160
00:09:12,804 --> 00:09:16,912
pack it as model.tar.gz,
and upload it to your bucket.

161
00:09:17,232 --> 00:09:20,966
Those are steps 1 and 3 of the lecture
deployment picture.

162
00:09:21,286 --> 00:09:24,656
Step 2, the signature change,
is not in part 1.

163
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:26,952
Pause and run it.

164
00:09:27,272 --> 00:09:29,215
Ours stops right away:

165
00:09:29,215 --> 00:09:34,781
no credentials, at line 73 of cli.py,
which creates the bucket.

166
00:09:35,101 --> 00:09:39,198
And the artifacts folder does not exist,
so nothing was downloaded.

167
00:09:39,434 --> 00:09:41,137
A small gap in the README:

168
00:09:41,137 --> 00:09:44,871
the code creates the bucket
before it downloads anything,

169
00:09:44,871 --> 00:09:47,007
a step the list does not mention.

170
00:09:47,327 --> 00:09:52,255
With your keys, it creates the bucket,
or reuses yours if it already exists.

171
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,671
To show what comes next, an extra:
the same prepare function,

172
00:09:56,671 --> 00:09:59,817
with S3 replaced by a stub
that sends nothing.

173
00:10:00,137 --> 00:10:03,207
The release zip downloads,
the model is exported,

174
00:10:03,207 --> 00:10:05,087
and the stub stops the upload.

175
00:10:05,407 --> 00:10:07,370
Nothing reaches AWS.

176
00:10:07,867 --> 00:10:09,211
Here is what it made.

177
00:10:09,531 --> 00:10:11,370
A SavedModel is a folder:

178
00:10:11,370 --> 00:10:15,417
the graph and its named entry
points in saved_model.pb,

179
00:10:15,417 --> 00:10:18,214
and the weights in a variables folder.

180
00:10:18,450 --> 00:10:20,953
The code puts it in a folder named 1,

181
00:10:20,953 --> 00:10:25,149
because TensorFlow Serving finds
models by name, then version.

182
00:10:25,469 --> 00:10:27,858
The tarball holds that folder at its top.

183
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:34,105
An extra git status shows your two edits,
and Git ignores the artifacts folder.

184
00:10:34,425 --> 00:10:39,546
In your account, the tarball now sits in S3,
and storage bills while it exists.

185
00:10:41,883 --> 00:10:43,419
Step 2: deploy.

186
00:10:43,739 --> 00:10:45,979
Remember the three pieces from the start.

187
00:10:46,299 --> 00:10:49,440
In code,
it first describes a TensorFlow model:

188
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:53,382
the tarball in S3 and the serving container,
plus the role.

189
00:10:53,702 --> 00:10:56,048
Nothing exists in AWS yet.

190
00:10:56,283 --> 00:10:58,950
The lecture uploads
to a model registry here.

191
00:10:59,270 --> 00:11:01,803
Both READMEs say model registry too,

192
00:11:01,803 --> 00:11:05,179
but the code creates a plain model
in SageMaker.

193
00:11:05,416 --> 00:11:07,927
You never write an image name:

194
00:11:07,927 --> 00:11:14,205
the SDK picks the AWS TensorFlow Serving
image from framework version 2.13.

195
00:11:14,525 --> 00:11:17,981
Our model was exported by TensorFlow 2.18.

196
00:11:18,301 --> 00:11:22,611
In step 3, a local check shows
that Serving 2.13 loads it.

197
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:27,234
Then deploy,
with one instance of type ml.m5.xlarge,

198
00:11:27,234 --> 00:11:29,762
under a name with a timestamp.

199
00:11:30,082 --> 00:11:32,211
That one call creates all three:

200
00:11:32,211 --> 00:11:35,537
the model in SageMaker,
an endpoint configuration,

201
00:11:35,537 --> 00:11:40,663
which names the machine and the model,
and the endpoint, the address you call.

202
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:44,697
Pause and run python cli.py --deploy.

203
00:11:45,017 --> 00:11:50,031
Ours stops at line 168,
the deploy call: no credentials.

204
00:11:50,351 --> 00:11:53,612
With yours, the README says 5 to 10 minutes,

205
00:11:53,612 --> 00:11:58,798
then it prints the endpoint name
and saves it in endpoint_config.json.

206
00:11:59,033 --> 00:12:02,937
The lecture calls this serverless,
meaning no server for you to manage.

207
00:12:03,257 --> 00:12:04,772
It is still one machine,

208
00:12:04,772 --> 00:12:07,865
billing around the clock,
even with zero traffic.

209
00:12:08,185 --> 00:12:11,562
Unlike Cloud Run in lecture 14,
this endpoint,

210
00:12:11,562 --> 00:12:15,012
as the code creates it,
does not scale to zero.

211
00:12:15,250 --> 00:12:17,383
The lecture compares two ways to serve.

212
00:12:17,703 --> 00:12:21,047
A model endpoint deploys
a trained model directly,

213
00:12:21,047 --> 00:12:24,658
with versioning,
A/B testing and monitoring available.

214
00:12:24,978 --> 00:12:28,544
A general container service
like Cloud Run runs anything,

215
00:12:28,544 --> 00:12:30,546
and you build the rest yourself.

216
00:12:30,783 --> 00:12:34,468
Its rule of thumb:
an endpoint to serve a trained model,

217
00:12:34,468 --> 00:12:38,612
a container service
when the model is one part of a larger app.

218
00:12:38,932 --> 00:12:40,895
Here, those features go unused.

219
00:12:42,233 --> 00:12:45,966
Step 3: python cli.py --predict.

220
00:12:46,286 --> 00:12:51,470
Ours finds no endpoint_config.json
and falls back to a default name.

221
00:12:51,790 --> 00:12:56,804
It picks five photos and fails five times:
Unable to locate credentials.

222
00:12:57,124 --> 00:12:58,596
Pause and run yours.

223
00:12:58,833 --> 00:13:00,753
Each try would send one photo.

224
00:13:01,073 --> 00:13:07,217
The client resizes it to 224 by 224,
with 3 color channels.

225
00:13:07,537 --> 00:13:10,993
How many numbers travel
to the endpoint for that one photo?

226
00:13:19,833 --> 00:13:27,513
224 times 224 times 3: 150,528 floats.

227
00:13:27,833 --> 00:13:32,569
Python writes each one as text in JSON,
so a few megabytes per photo.

228
00:13:32,889 --> 00:13:36,409
A signature
that takes bytes would send the JPEG itself.

229
00:13:36,650 --> 00:13:39,317
The answer is a list of four probabilities.

230
00:13:39,637 --> 00:13:43,951
The code takes the largest with argmax
and looks it up:

231
00:13:43,951 --> 00:13:47,167
0 parmigiano, 1 gruyere, 2 brie, 3 gouda.

232
00:13:47,416 --> 00:13:48,988
To see a real answer,

233
00:13:48,988 --> 00:13:52,429
we use an extra local stand-in,
not SageMaker.

234
00:13:52,749 --> 00:13:56,420
The AWS serving image
runs TensorFlow Serving,

235
00:13:56,420 --> 00:14:01,048
so we started the public
Serving 2.13 image on our export.

236
00:14:01,368 --> 00:14:02,862
It loads version 1.

237
00:14:03,100 --> 00:14:06,961
Then the same predict function
sends the same JSON to it.

238
00:14:07,281 --> 00:14:08,531
Brie comes back brie,

239
00:14:08,531 --> 00:14:11,505
and gruyere comes back gruyere,
four numbers each.

240
00:14:11,750 --> 00:14:15,249
And parmigiano comes back brie,
at 98 percent.

241
00:14:15,569 --> 00:14:18,385
That is a real mistake
of the released model.

242
00:14:18,705 --> 00:14:22,864
Its own metrics file reports
about 72 percent accuracy.

243
00:14:23,100 --> 00:14:24,615
Now the opening puzzle.

244
00:14:24,935 --> 00:14:29,685
Same code, different artifact:
the README output came from an older model,

245
00:14:29,685 --> 00:14:32,444
with three classes and another label order.

246
00:14:32,764 --> 00:14:37,308
A small gap: expect four numbers,
and index 0 is parmigiano.

247
00:14:37,550 --> 00:14:40,046
An extra command removes the stand-in.

248
00:14:40,366 --> 00:14:42,328
It never touched AWS.

249
00:14:43,567 --> 00:14:47,455
The lecture warns
that an endpoint costs money while it is up,

250
00:14:47,455 --> 00:14:49,839
and the slides shout one word: delete.

251
00:14:50,159 --> 00:14:51,844
The README gives three ways.

252
00:14:52,164 --> 00:14:56,730
Any one stops the endpoint,
but only the third also removes the tarball.

253
00:14:57,050 --> 00:15:01,466
Way 1, the console:
SageMaker, Endpoints, select yours, Delete.

254
00:15:01,767 --> 00:15:04,924
Way 2 gives three AWS CLI commands.

255
00:15:05,244 --> 00:15:08,820
Typed exactly as written,
the first one is a shell error:

256
00:15:08,820 --> 00:15:13,778
the angle brackets are placeholders,
and the shell takes them as a redirection.

257
00:15:14,017 --> 00:15:16,961
A small gap:
type your own names, without brackets.

258
00:15:17,281 --> 00:15:19,777
Ours then fails: no credentials.

259
00:15:20,097 --> 00:15:24,790
Delete in this order: the endpoint,
then its configuration, then the model.

260
00:15:26,567 --> 00:15:32,626
Way 3: python cli.py --delete stops
at its first SageMaker call.

261
00:15:32,946 --> 00:15:34,098
Pause and run it.

262
00:15:34,418 --> 00:15:38,279
With keys, it deletes the endpoint
named in the JSON file.

263
00:15:38,599 --> 00:15:41,979
Then come matching configurations
and the model,

264
00:15:41,979 --> 00:15:45,148
by naming convention,
then the tarball in S3.

265
00:15:45,384 --> 00:15:49,053
Each deploy makes a new endpoint
with a new timestamp,

266
00:15:49,053 --> 00:15:51,976
but the JSON file keeps only the last name.

267
00:15:52,296 --> 00:15:54,813
You ran deploy twice, then delete once.

268
00:15:55,133 --> 00:15:56,840
How many endpoints still bill?

269
00:16:03,684 --> 00:16:07,737
One. Delete removes only
the endpoint the file names.

270
00:16:08,057 --> 00:16:09,721
The older one keeps running.

271
00:16:10,041 --> 00:16:14,798
Check with aws sagemaker list-endpoints,
and confirm in the console.

272
00:16:15,034 --> 00:16:16,250
That is part 1.

273
00:16:16,570 --> 00:16:19,791
You ran four commands
by hand to deploy one model.

274
00:16:20,111 --> 00:16:22,735
The Cheese App has four stages before that.

275
00:16:23,055 --> 00:16:26,170
Who runs all of them,
in the right order, every time?

